<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Etruscans</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 900–1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> · Also known as Etruscan civilisation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Etruscans were the people of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, in what is now Tuscany, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, whose civilisation flourished from about 900 BCE. They spoke a non-Indo-European language written in an alphabet adapted from the Greek, lived in independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> such as Veii, Tarquinia and Caere, and traded across the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> absorbed their cities by the 3rd century BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/etruscans.jpg" alt="Etruscans" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NormanEinstein · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan_civilization_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Literary texts in Etruscan have not survived and the language is only partly understood, so what is known about the Etruscans depends heavily on later and generally disapproving Roman and Greek writers. These writers differed about the origin of the people, some holding that they were native to central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and others that they were Pelasgians. The consensus among modern scholars is that the Etruscans were an indigenous population. The earliest identifiably Etruscan culture dates from about 900 BCE, in the Iron Age Villanovan culture. According to Dionysius of Halicarnassus they called themselves Rasenna, from Rasna, the people, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called them Tyrrhenians, which gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a> its name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the last Villanovan phase, about 770 to 730 BCE, the Etruscans entered close relations with the first Greek settlers in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and took over writing, a new way of banqueting and a heroic funerary ideology. The earliest known Etruscan inscriptions date to about 700 BCE, in a script derived from the Euboean alphabet. The mining and trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and iron enriched them, and in the sixth century BCE their interests clashed with Greek colonists, so that they allied themselves with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. According to legend, a league of 12 cities formed between 600 and 500 BCE.</p><p>Their territory reached its fullest extent about 500 BCE, shortly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-kingdom">Roman Kingdom</a> became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>. The conflict between Veii and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> began in 482 BCE, and from the late 4th century BCE the Etruscans succumbed to Rome in the Roman–Etruscan Wars. They were granted Roman citizenship in 90 BCE, and by 27 BCE the whole territory belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Authority lay with individual cities and probably with individual families, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> was the political unit. The government is thought to have changed from monarchy to an oligarchic republic in the sixth century BCE. The married couple stood at the centre of a monogamous society, and the great family tombs held members of a family over long periods. Elite families grew rich through trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> to the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> to the south, and filled their tombs with imported luxuries. The economy rested on agriculture, mining and metalworking, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> trade; Populonia was a major centre of iron smelting.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Etruscan civilization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Etruscans</b> called themselves Rasna, 'the people'; the league's annual assembly chose a zilath mechl rasnal, a 'magistrate of the Etruscan people', and inscriptions speak of the Mechlum Rasnal, the 'community of the people'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Etruscans</b> believed in intimate contact with divinity and did nothing without consulting the gods and seeking signs from them.</blockquote><a href="/religion/etruscan-religion">Etruscan religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Through the <b>Etruscans</b> it gave rise to the Latin alphabet, now the most widely used in the world; around 900 the Cyrillic alphabet was created for the Slavs, largely from Greek letters; and it inspired the Coptic, Gothic and Armenian scripts.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early centuries of the first millennium BCE it was one language among many in Italy, overshadowed by the <b>Etruscans</b> to the north and by the Greek cities of the south.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…by Phoenician trade to the Maghreb and Europe, it was adopted by the Greeks, then adapted by the <b>Etruscans</b>, whose version the Romans modified into the Latin alphabet; it became the source of all modern European scripts and is generally believed to be at least a partial ancestor…</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>A 2024 study, still a preprint, found that the steppe ancestry of Beaker populations came from the Corded Ware culture rather than directly from the Yamnaya culture, and Beaker-related ancestry contributed to later peoples of Italy, including the Iron Age <b>Etruscans</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/bell-beaker-culture">Bell Beaker culture · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
